1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,184
It is three in the morning.

2
00:00:02,184 --> 00:00:03,306
Your shop is slow,

3
00:00:03,306 --> 00:00:05,177
and some customers see errors.

4
00:00:05,177 --> 00:00:06,736
Who finds out first: you,

5
00:00:06,736 --> 00:00:07,796
or the customers?

6
00:00:08,346 --> 00:00:10,423
A program that runs is not enough.

7
00:00:10,423 --> 00:00:12,073
You must know: is it alive,

8
00:00:12,073 --> 00:00:12,806
is it ready,

9
00:00:12,806 --> 00:00:13,844
what is it doing,

10
00:00:13,844 --> 00:00:15,066
and why did it fail?

11
00:00:15,616 --> 00:00:17,458
Today you learn five ideas.

12
00:00:17,458 --> 00:00:18,414
Health checks.

13
00:00:18,414 --> 00:00:19,096
Readiness.

14
00:00:19,096 --> 00:00:19,437
Logs.

15
00:00:19,437 --> 00:00:19,983
Metrics.

16
00:00:19,983 --> 00:00:24,213
And how an error travels from the database up to the customer.

17
00:00:24,763 --> 00:00:26,927
You will break the database on purpose,

18
00:00:26,927 --> 00:00:29,256
and watch the shop tell you what is wrong,

19
00:00:29,256 --> 00:00:30,310
with real commands.

20
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,475
Go into the shop. Type: CD, cloud cart.

21
00:00:36,025 --> 00:00:39,075
Start it. Type: dot slash start dot S H.

22
00:00:39,625 --> 00:00:40,724
A load balancer,

23
00:00:40,724 --> 00:00:42,716
or a tool such as Kubernetes,

24
00:00:42,716 --> 00:00:44,913
cannot look inside your program.

25
00:00:44,913 --> 00:00:47,866
So the program answers two small questions,

26
00:00:47,866 --> 00:00:51,231
and a script called probe dot S H asks them both.

27
00:00:51,781 --> 00:00:53,978
Type: dot slash probe dot S H.

28
00:00:56,028 --> 00:00:57,906
Health Z asks: are you alive?

29
00:00:57,906 --> 00:01:00,756
Ready Z asks: can you do your job right now?

30
00:01:00,756 --> 00:01:02,310
Both answer two hundred,

31
00:01:02,310 --> 00:01:03,281
so all is well.

32
00:01:03,831 --> 00:01:05,765
Now break something on purpose.

33
00:01:05,765 --> 00:01:08,822
The shop keeps its products in one database file.

34
00:01:08,822 --> 00:01:10,070
Move that file away,

35
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:11,255
like a failed disk.

36
00:01:11,805 --> 00:01:15,517
Type: M V, shop dot D B, shop dot D B dot off.

37
00:01:17,067 --> 00:01:21,462
Ask both questions again. Type: dot slash probe dot S H.

38
00:01:23,512 --> 00:01:27,011
Look. Health Z still says two hundred: the program is alive.

39
00:01:27,561 --> 00:01:29,427
But ready Z says five oh three,

40
00:01:29,427 --> 00:01:30,029
not ready,

41
00:01:30,029 --> 00:01:32,979
and it names the check that failed: the database.

42
00:01:33,529 --> 00:01:34,690
Why two questions?

43
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:37,270
Because the right reaction is different.

44
00:01:37,270 --> 00:01:40,236
A program that is not alive must be restarted.

45
00:01:40,236 --> 00:01:44,105
A program that is alive but not ready must not be restarted.

46
00:01:44,105 --> 00:01:47,652
You simply stop sending it customers until it recovers.

47
00:01:48,202 --> 00:01:53,301
Put the file back. Type: M V, shop dot D B dot off, shop dot D B.

48
00:01:54,851 --> 00:01:58,413
And ask again. Type: dot slash probe dot S H.

49
00:02:00,463 --> 00:02:04,346
Ready again. Customers flow back. Nobody restarted anything.

50
00:02:06,396 --> 00:02:09,511
Go into the shop. Type: CD, cloud cart.

51
00:02:10,061 --> 00:02:13,111
Start it. Type: dot slash start dot S H.

52
00:02:13,661 --> 00:02:17,798
Now the real danger: the database fails while customers are shopping.

53
00:02:17,798 --> 00:02:19,417
What does the customer see,

54
00:02:19,417 --> 00:02:20,616
and what do you see?

55
00:02:21,166 --> 00:02:26,158
Break it again. Type: M V, shop dot D B, shop dot D B dot off.

56
00:02:27,708 --> 00:02:29,759
A customer asks for the products.

57
00:02:29,759 --> 00:02:31,810
We label the request with an I D,

58
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:33,425
so we can follow it later.

59
00:02:33,975 --> 00:02:34,793
Type: curl,

60
00:02:34,793 --> 00:02:35,313
dash s,

61
00:02:35,313 --> 00:02:36,428
dash capital H,

62
00:02:36,428 --> 00:02:37,171
in quotes,

63
00:02:37,171 --> 00:02:38,211
X request I D,

64
00:02:38,211 --> 00:02:38,657
colon,

65
00:02:38,657 --> 00:02:39,772
demo forty-two,

66
00:02:39,772 --> 00:02:41,035
then the address,

67
00:02:41,035 --> 00:02:42,893
slash API slash products.

68
00:02:45,243 --> 00:02:46,960
The customer gets a short,

69
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:49,339
clean message: database unavailable,

70
00:02:49,339 --> 00:02:50,660
and the request I D.

71
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:51,387
No secrets,

72
00:02:51,387 --> 00:02:51,982
no crash,

73
00:02:51,982 --> 00:02:53,039
no wall of code.

74
00:02:53,039 --> 00:02:55,615
The error was caught where it happened,

75
00:02:55,615 --> 00:02:58,192
and turned into something safe to show.

76
00:02:58,742 --> 00:03:03,265
The details go in the log, not to the customer. Search the log for that I D.

77
00:03:03,815 --> 00:03:06,780
Type: grep, demo forty-two, API dot log.

78
00:03:08,830 --> 00:03:09,433
One line.

79
00:03:09,433 --> 00:03:10,035
The time,

80
00:03:10,035 --> 00:03:11,107
the level error,

81
00:03:11,107 --> 00:03:12,781
the status five oh three,

82
00:03:12,781 --> 00:03:13,584
the request,

83
00:03:13,584 --> 00:03:14,120
the I D,

84
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:16,396
and the real cause: no such table,

85
00:03:16,396 --> 00:03:16,999
products.

86
00:03:16,999 --> 00:03:20,414
The customer's I D leads you straight to the cause.

87
00:03:20,964 --> 00:03:21,967
Now repair it.

88
00:03:22,517 --> 00:03:26,186
Type: M V, shop dot D B dot off, shop dot D B.

89
00:03:27,736 --> 00:03:29,473
And ask for product two.

90
00:03:29,473 --> 00:03:30,270
Type: curl,

91
00:03:30,270 --> 00:03:30,776
dash s,

92
00:03:30,776 --> 00:03:31,645
the address,

93
00:03:31,645 --> 00:03:34,179
slash API slash products slash two.

94
00:03:36,229 --> 00:03:37,163
Working again.

95
00:03:37,163 --> 00:03:41,900
This is error propagation: a failure deep in the system travels upward.

96
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:42,500
Catch it,

97
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:43,101
label it,

98
00:03:43,101 --> 00:03:44,035
log the cause,

99
00:03:44,035 --> 00:03:46,503
and show the customer a safe message.

100
00:03:46,503 --> 00:03:48,571
Never let the raw error escape,

101
00:03:48,571 --> 00:03:50,373
and never hide it silently.

102
00:03:52,423 --> 00:03:55,538
Go into the shop. Type: CD, cloud cart.

103
00:03:56,088 --> 00:03:59,138
Start it. Type: dot slash start dot S H.

104
00:03:59,688 --> 00:04:04,456
A log is the diary of your program: one line for each thing that happened.

105
00:04:04,456 --> 00:04:07,549
Let us create some history with three customers.

106
00:04:07,549 --> 00:04:08,837
One finds a product,

107
00:04:08,837 --> 00:04:10,835
one asks for a missing product,

108
00:04:10,835 --> 00:04:12,510
and one forgets to log in.

109
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:17,561
Type: curl, dash s, the address, slash API slash products slash two.

110
00:04:19,611 --> 00:04:19,960
Next,

111
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:22,051
a product that does not exist.

112
00:04:22,051 --> 00:04:22,819
Type: curl,

113
00:04:22,819 --> 00:04:23,307
dash s,

114
00:04:23,307 --> 00:04:24,143
the address,

115
00:04:24,143 --> 00:04:27,142
slash API slash products slash ninety-nine.

116
00:04:29,192 --> 00:04:30,222
And the orders,

117
00:04:30,222 --> 00:04:31,526
without logging in.

118
00:04:31,526 --> 00:04:32,281
Type: curl,

119
00:04:32,281 --> 00:04:32,762
dash s,

120
00:04:32,762 --> 00:04:33,586
the address,

121
00:04:33,586 --> 00:04:35,165
slash API slash orders.

122
00:04:37,215 --> 00:04:40,714
Now read the diary. Type: cat, API dot log.

123
00:04:42,764 --> 00:04:46,390
Info means normal: the product was served, status two hundred.

124
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:48,485
Warn means something odd,

125
00:04:48,485 --> 00:04:51,142
usually the customer's doing: four oh four,

126
00:04:51,142 --> 00:04:52,255
a missing product,

127
00:04:52,255 --> 00:04:53,243
and four oh one,

128
00:04:53,243 --> 00:04:54,108
not logged in.

129
00:04:54,658 --> 00:04:56,740
Every line says when it happened,

130
00:04:56,740 --> 00:04:57,939
how serious it was,

131
00:04:57,939 --> 00:04:59,264
what the request was,

132
00:04:59,264 --> 00:05:01,094
and which request I D it had.

133
00:05:01,094 --> 00:05:01,536
Errors,

134
00:05:01,536 --> 00:05:02,608
at the top level,

135
00:05:02,608 --> 00:05:05,069
mean we failed: the five hundred class.

136
00:05:05,619 --> 00:05:07,609
Good logs follow a few rules.

137
00:05:07,609 --> 00:05:09,256
One line for each event.

138
00:05:09,256 --> 00:05:11,452
Always a time and a request I D.

139
00:05:11,452 --> 00:05:13,237
Never passwords or tokens.

140
00:05:13,237 --> 00:05:15,501
And send them to a central place,

141
00:05:15,501 --> 00:05:18,590
because a server can disappear with its logs.

142
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:23,755
Go into the shop. Type: CD, cloud cart.

143
00:05:24,305 --> 00:05:27,355
Start it. Type: dot slash start dot S H.

144
00:05:27,905 --> 00:05:29,663
Logs describe single events.

145
00:05:29,663 --> 00:05:31,859
Metrics describe the whole picture,

146
00:05:31,859 --> 00:05:33,742
in numbers: how many requests,

147
00:05:33,742 --> 00:05:34,747
how many failed,

148
00:05:34,747 --> 00:05:36,567
how long it has been running.

149
00:05:37,117 --> 00:05:42,179
A small script called traffic dot S H plays five customers: three good requests,

150
00:05:42,179 --> 00:05:43,445
one missing product,

151
00:05:43,445 --> 00:05:46,482
and one that arrives while the database is down.

152
00:05:47,032 --> 00:05:49,400
Type: dot slash traffic dot S H.

153
00:05:51,750 --> 00:05:53,585
Now ask the shop for its numbers.

154
00:05:54,135 --> 00:05:57,057
Type: curl, dash s, the address, slash metrics.

155
00:05:59,107 --> 00:06:00,693
Five requests in total.

156
00:06:00,693 --> 00:06:02,417
Counters only ever go up.

157
00:06:02,417 --> 00:06:07,589
A monitoring tool such as Prometheus reads these numbers every few seconds,

158
00:06:07,589 --> 00:06:08,486
draws graphs,

159
00:06:08,486 --> 00:06:11,865
and sends an alert when something grows too fast.

160
00:06:12,415 --> 00:06:13,487
Three succeeded.

161
00:06:13,487 --> 00:06:14,761
One was a four X X,

162
00:06:14,761 --> 00:06:16,168
the customer's doing.

163
00:06:16,168 --> 00:06:18,581
And one was a five X X: our failure.

164
00:06:18,581 --> 00:06:21,598
One failure in five is twenty percent errors,

165
00:06:21,598 --> 00:06:23,743
and that would wake somebody up.

166
00:06:24,293 --> 00:06:25,927
You can filter for it.

167
00:06:25,927 --> 00:06:26,744
Type: curl,

168
00:06:26,744 --> 00:06:27,264
dash s,

169
00:06:27,264 --> 00:06:28,156
the address,

170
00:06:28,156 --> 00:06:29,196
slash metrics,

171
00:06:29,196 --> 00:06:29,567
pipe,

172
00:06:29,567 --> 00:06:29,939
grep,

173
00:06:29,939 --> 00:06:30,607
five X X.

174
00:06:32,657 --> 00:06:33,641
The same number,

175
00:06:33,641 --> 00:06:34,318
on its own.

176
00:06:34,318 --> 00:06:36,840
Remember it like this: logs tell you why.

177
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:38,439
Metrics tell you how much.

178
00:06:40,489 --> 00:06:42,174
Let us put the ideas together.

179
00:06:42,724 --> 00:06:47,439
A health check answers one question: is the program alive? If not, restart it.

180
00:06:47,989 --> 00:06:51,350
A readiness check asks: can it do its job right now?

181
00:06:51,350 --> 00:06:51,802
If not,

182
00:06:51,802 --> 00:06:53,289
stop sending customers,

183
00:06:53,289 --> 00:06:54,517
but do not restart.

184
00:06:55,067 --> 00:06:59,760
Logs are the diary: events, each with a time, a level, and a request I D.

185
00:07:00,310 --> 00:07:02,647
Metrics are the numbers: requests,

186
00:07:02,647 --> 00:07:03,128
errors,

187
00:07:03,128 --> 00:07:04,846
and how long things take.

188
00:07:04,846 --> 00:07:06,633
Teams watch four: latency,

189
00:07:06,633 --> 00:07:07,182
traffic,

190
00:07:07,182 --> 00:07:07,664
errors,

191
00:07:07,664 --> 00:07:08,694
and saturation.

192
00:07:09,244 --> 00:07:11,627
Error propagation: catch the failure,

193
00:07:11,627 --> 00:07:12,786
add a request I D,

194
00:07:12,786 --> 00:07:13,688
log the cause,

195
00:07:13,688 --> 00:07:16,071
and show the customer a safe message.

196
00:07:16,621 --> 00:07:20,514
A request I D travels with the request through every service,

197
00:07:20,514 --> 00:07:24,088
so one label connects a customer's report to a log line.

198
00:07:24,638 --> 00:07:25,790
In real systems,

199
00:07:25,790 --> 00:07:27,734
Kubernetes runs the probes,

200
00:07:27,734 --> 00:07:30,038
Prometheus collects the metrics,

201
00:07:30,038 --> 00:07:32,702
and a log platform collects the logs.

202
00:07:32,702 --> 00:07:34,430
The ideas stay the same.

203
00:07:36,480 --> 00:07:39,253
Your task, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

204
00:07:39,803 --> 00:07:42,487
The lab is lab seventy-three dot S H,

205
00:07:42,487 --> 00:07:46,186
in the academy slash lab folder of git hub dot com,

206
00:07:46,186 --> 00:07:47,637
slash it dash waves,

207
00:07:47,637 --> 00:07:49,305
slash dev ops dash A I.

208
00:07:49,305 --> 00:07:50,973
Install it like before,

209
00:07:50,973 --> 00:07:51,771
run lab up,

210
00:07:51,771 --> 00:07:55,398
and work inside slash opt slash lab seventy-three.

211
00:07:55,948 --> 00:08:01,324
One: with the shop healthy, run probe dot S H and save the output in up dot text.

212
00:08:01,874 --> 00:08:03,981
Two: move shop dot D B away,

213
00:08:03,981 --> 00:08:05,788
run probe dot S H again,

214
00:08:05,788 --> 00:08:08,573
and save the output in down dot text.

215
00:08:09,123 --> 00:08:13,221
Three: send one request with the I D lab dash seventy-three,

216
00:08:13,221 --> 00:08:16,568
and save its line from the log in R I D dot text.

217
00:08:17,118 --> 00:08:20,190
Four: save the metrics page in metrics dot text.

218
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:22,387
Five: in answers dot text,

219
00:08:22,387 --> 00:08:24,605
write which probe means restart it,

220
00:08:24,605 --> 00:08:27,076
and which means stop sending customers.

221
00:08:27,626 --> 00:08:29,426
Six: put the database back,

222
00:08:29,426 --> 00:08:31,226
so the shop is ready again.

223
00:08:31,226 --> 00:08:32,493
Then run lab check.

224
00:08:32,493 --> 00:08:33,493
Six pass lines.

225
00:08:35,543 --> 00:08:37,527
Six points, and you need all six.

226
00:08:38,077 --> 00:08:39,912
The checker shows six pass lines.

227
00:08:40,462 --> 00:08:42,595
You can explain alive against ready.

228
00:08:43,145 --> 00:08:46,686
You can follow one request I D from the customer to the log.

229
00:08:47,236 --> 00:08:50,522
You can read the five X X counter, and say what it means.

230
00:08:51,072 --> 00:08:55,658
You can say what a customer should see when the database fails, and where the cause goes.

231
00:08:56,208 --> 00:08:58,939
You ran lab down, and nothing is left listening.

232
00:08:59,489 --> 00:09:01,110
To pass, you need all six.

233
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:05,997
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

234
00:09:06,547 --> 00:09:08,766
One: install the lab, and run lab up.

235
00:09:09,316 --> 00:09:13,263
Two: probe the healthy shop, then break the database, and probe again.

236
00:09:13,813 --> 00:09:17,525
Three: send a request with your own I D, and find it in the log.

237
00:09:18,075 --> 00:09:19,611
Four: save the metrics.

238
00:09:20,161 --> 00:09:23,105
Five: write your answers, and put the database back.

239
00:09:23,655 --> 00:09:25,937
Six: run lab check, then lab down.

240
00:09:26,487 --> 00:09:28,322
Pause the video, and begin now.

241
00:09:43,872 --> 00:09:44,747
Quick check.

242
00:09:45,297 --> 00:09:47,537
Alive and ready: what is the difference?

243
00:09:48,087 --> 00:09:49,857
What does a request I D connect?

244
00:09:50,407 --> 00:09:53,287
Logs or metrics: which one tells you how much?

245
00:09:53,837 --> 00:09:56,376
What should the customer see when the database fails?

246
00:09:56,926 --> 00:09:59,433
Objective covered: M C zero eight,

247
00:09:59,433 --> 00:10:01,203
objective seven: health,

248
00:10:01,203 --> 00:10:01,940
readiness,

249
00:10:01,940 --> 00:10:02,309
logs,

250
00:10:02,309 --> 00:10:02,899
metrics,

251
00:10:02,899 --> 00:10:04,521
and error propagation.

252
00:10:05,071 --> 00:10:07,140
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

253
00:10:17,690 --> 00:10:21,530
Next: draw the whole shop, and trace one request through every part.

254
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:25,408
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
